2026-01-15 18:02:50,353 [INFO] root - [3] 来源: Tensile and Low Cycle Fatigue Behaviour of Cast Superalloy Supercast 247 A in Polycrystalline Condition (块: None)
Indigenously developed SUPERCAST 247 A is a nickel base cast super alloy, nearly equivalent to CM
247 LC. It is intended for the manufacture of critical aero-engine hot end components that include
high pressure turbine blades and vanes, Integral Rotors and Stators of Jet fuel starter of an
indigenous aircraft. The present study intends to evaluate the temperature dependent tensile and low
cycle fatigue properties of this alloy in the range of 25 to 650°C as a function of microstructure
that is obtainable in two specific heat treatment conditions. Gamma prime size and its morphology
varied with the heat treatment conditions. Its size is finer in Solutionising + Single Aging (SSA)
condition as compared to that in Solutionising + Double Aging (SDA) condition which reflected in
high tensile strength values of the material in SSA condition. The alloy in SSA condition exhibited
good fatigue resistance at room temperature whereas the alloy in SDA condition showed good fatigue
resistance at 650°C.

2026-01-15 18:02:50,353 [INFO] root - [4] 来源: Strengthened superalloy for gas turbine blades (块: None)
One of the greatest technological challenges to the metallurgist is the design of alloys for gas-
turbine blades. These components are subjected to high rotational stresses and must operate in
aggressive environments with flame temperatures often above the alloy melting point. Today worldwide
activity on improving turbine blade alloys includes investigation into single crystal melting
technology, intermetallic compounds and PM manufacturing methods. Godfrey Hack of Inco Alloys of
Hereford, UK, describes his company's process of mechanical alloying for the production of oxide
dispersion strengthened superalloys for the blades in commercial gas turbines.

2026-01-15 18:02:50,353 [INFO] root - [5] 来源: The influence of high temperature on DV-2 jet engine Ni-based superalloy turbine blade degradation (块: None)
Blades and vanes made from Ni – and Co – base superalloys that are used in the hot section of all
gas turbine engines are coated to enhance resistance to hot corrosion. The most widely used coatings
are those based on the intermetallic compounds NiAl and CoAl, which are formed by diffusion
interaction of aluminium with surfaces of the nickel and cobalt alloys, respectively. Simple
aluminide coatings resist high – temperature oxidation by the formation of protective layer of
alumina and can be used up to about 1,150°C. The coatings degrade by loss of aluminium due to
spalling of oxides under thermal cycling conditions. HPT blades of DV – 2 jet engines are made from
Ni – based superalloy ŽS6K. For improving alloy’s high temperature resistance are blades coated with
Al – Si diffusion layer. Depending on jet engine flying regime an overcrossing of working
temperature may occur. It may result into coarsening of the gamma prime particles, thus decreasing
its strengthening effect. A real case study of DV-2 jet engine turbine blades after high temperature
exposition is discussed in this article.

