A manufacturing process for dual-structured turbine disks made from PM nickel-base superalloys has
been specially developed. The disks are composed of two metals: AF115, which has superior stress
rupture properties, was used in the rim region, and TMP-3, which has good tensile properties and a
low cycle-fatigue life, was used in the bore region. A 400mm diameter dual-property disk was
successfully produced through double-stepped HIP treatment and superplastic forging, in which gas
atomized powders composed of the above alloys were consolidated into a well-designed preformed
structure. After heat treatment, the microstructure of the product was very fine and homogeneous in
the bore region, and coarse grains were obtained in the rim region. The mechanical properties were
of such high quality as to enable practical use as a turbine disk.

