2026-01-15 18:56:35,623 [INFO] root - [8] 来源: Precipitation and transformation of highly dispersed α-Cr phase in Ni3Al-based superalloy (块: None)
Precipitation and transformation of α-Cr phase in Ni3Al-based alloy was studied by using aging heat
treatment. The results showed that clustered and highly dispersed α-Cr phase precipitated at later
aging stage from γ matrix of the alloy which was solution treated with air cooling (SAA) and water
cooling (SWA), respectively. Orientation relationship between α-Cr phase and γ′ was
[−110]α−Cr∥[−110]γ′. Due to the fastest cooling rate of SWA, plenty of vacancies formed which
contributed to the precipitation of highly dispersed α-Cr phase. Supersaturation of Cr element under
SWA was lower than that of SAA, resulting in the delay of α-Cr phase nucleation under SWA compared
with SAA. Chromium-rich γ matrix directly decompose into α-Cr phase, strengthening the Ni3Al-based
alloy by impeding dislocation motion. In addition, no α-Cr phase precipitated from γ matrix during
aging after solution treatment with furnace cooling (SFA) because the precipitation of secondary γ′
between adjacent primary γ′(γI′) during furnace cooling (SF) which consumed Cr elements. Moreover,
γI′ grows directionally with aging time prolonging under SAA and SWA, whereas it remains concave
cube under SFA.

2026-01-15 18:56:35,624 [INFO] root - [9] 来源: Near-Surface and Bulk Dissolution Behavior of γ?Precipitates in Nickel-Based VDM<sup>®</sup> Alloy 780 Studied with In-Situ Lab-Source and Synchrotron X-ray Diffraction (块: None)
The dissolution of nano-sized Ni3Al-based γ?precipitates was investigated in the newly developed
polycrystalline nickel-based VDM<sup>®</sup> Alloy 780 at the surface and in the bulk region with
in-situ lab-source and synchrotron X-ray diffraction. These studies are important in obtaining a
deeper understanding of the strengthening mechanism responsible for the stability and long service
lives of such superalloys. We found that the dissolution behavior of the γ?phase is very similar at
the surface and in the bulk region, but small deviations were detected. The dissolution of γ?starts
at around 800 °C and no γ?was found at temperatures exceeding 970 °C. As a result, the elements Al
and Nb, which were bound in the γ?phase, dissolved into the γ matrix and strongly increased the γ
lattice parameter, as their atomic size is larger than the γ-forming elements Ni, Co, and Cr.
However, this effect was suppressed in the surface area. A second matrix γ phase was detected at the
same temperature range as that of the dissolution of the γ?phase in the lab-source XRD measurements.
The newly formed γ-2 phase had a smaller lattice parameter than that of the initial γ matrix. We
propose that the γ-2 matrix phase is a result of high-temperature surface oxidation, which consumes,
among other elements, Al and Nb and, therefore, leads to the smaller γ lattice parameter.

