ecame apparent in the 1970s due to the development of alloys such as Rene 95, Astroloy and MERL 76
(with strength levels higher than earlier alloys such IN718 and Waspaloy), which were found to be
unamenable to processing into the cast-and-wrought form. Initially, the use of P/M product was
limited to gas turbines for military purposes, for example, the Pratt & Whitney F100 engine for the
F-15 Eagle fighter, which entered service in 1974 [13], but commercial applications such as General
Electric's CF6-80 engine for the Boeing 747-400 and the GE90 for the Boeing 777 have emerged as
