One of the greatest technological challenges to the metallurgist is the design of alloys for gas-
turbine blades. These components are subjected to high rotational stresses and must operate in
aggressive environments with flame temperatures often above the alloy melting point. Today worldwide
activity on improving turbine blade alloys includes investigation into single crystal melting
technology, intermetallic compounds and PM manufacturing methods. Godfrey Hack of Inco Alloys of
Hereford, UK, describes his company's process of mechanical alloying for the production of oxide
dispersion strengthened superalloys for the blades in commercial gas turbines.

