In order to improve the high temperature wear- and oxidation resistance of single crystal
superalloy, NiCr-Cr3C2 coatings were vacuum brazed on the surface. Different weight ratios of Cr
were added into the coatings to control the diffusion of Si and C into the base metal, which would
be harmful to the base metal stability. The results show that, when no Cr was added into the
coatings, a layer of Ni3Si phase at the interface and MC precipitates in base metal were produced.
With the increase of the Cr addition, the coating/base metal interface was gradually purified. The
Ni3Si layer and the MC precipitates disappeared when the Cr addition increased to 30wt% and an
interface was clearly presented with the γ′ phases on both sides epitaxially growing on some extent.
After 200 thermal fatigue cycles, the total crack lengths were measured, which decreased with
increasing Cr addition, indicating that the thermal fatigue resistance was improved.

