The yield stresses of the turbine disc alloys correlate very strongly with the proportions of the
strengthening phases γ' and y", and thus a first overriding concern when designing a turbine disc
alloy is the choice of the concentration of elements which promote these phases, such that the
desired strength levels are achieved. One can see this by using thermodynamic software such as
Thermo-Calc to estimate the fractions of y' and y" present in the alloys given in Table 4.1, and
comparing the predictions with mechanical property data. The results, see Figure 4.17, confirm that
the yield stress at 650 °C correlates very strongly with the sum of the fractions of the
strengthening phases; the yield stress at ambient temperature behaves in the same way. The creep
performance, for example the stress for 1000 h life at  700°C, shows a similar correlation; see
Figure 4.18. These findings have bee

