Cyclic hysteresis loops generated during high-temperature, isothermal, low-cycle fatigue testing of
the nickel-based superalloy RR1000 revealed a gradual decrease in elastic modulus of up to 25 %
compared to the initial value in dependence of the accumulated plastic strain. Based on the
experimental observations, a material model was developed and implemented within a finite element
solver to investigate the effect of degrading stiffness on crack growth laws and crack growth
predictions. It was shown that the stress intensity factor diminished by 6 to 8 % and crack growth
rates were 14 to 20 % higher when stiffness degradation was accounted for.

