During long-term thermal exposure M23C6 dendritically precipitates from the supersaturated γ matrix
and then changes in shape from flower-like dendrite to irregular block to regular polyhedron. The
reduction in the interfacial energy is responsible for the morphological transition, but the stable
morphology of the carbide is principally determined by the anisotropy of the interfacial energy
between the carbide and the γ' in the M23C6-γ' cell. In addition, M23C6 has a negligible influence
on the tensile property of the thermally exposed alloy even though it can block the mobile
dislocations and stacking faults to some extent.

