During aging at 900 °C for 100 h of IN740H Ni-based superalloy, unexpected needle-like MC carbide
formed at or near incoherent twin boundary having a twin relationship with surrounding matrix.
Similar to the known needle-like M23C6 precipitation, the nucleation and growth of MC carbide in
needle-like morphology resulted from emission of Shockley partial dislocations and inherent
directional stress field originated from incoherent twin boundary, respectively. Also, this
precipitation process essentially gave rise to the twin relationship with its surrounding matrix.

