Complex thermally grown oxides (TGOs) are formed on a single-crystal Ni-based superalloy due to a
large fraction of alloying elements. Combining atom-probe tomography and transmission electron
microscopy analyses, the three-dimensional elemental distributions of metal oxides of the spinel and
refractory metal-oxide regions are presented. A spinel-type oxide, Ni(Cr,Al)2O4, is formed with a
crystallographic orientation relationship with the alloy substrate, which decomposes into other
oxides. The sequence of oxides observed in the TGO are NiO→Cr2O3→Ta2O5→NiTaO4,
NiTa2O6→Ni(Cr,Al)2O4→Al2O3.

