The deformation and damage mechanisms of a single crystal nickel-based superalloy containing
6.0%Re/5.0%Ru were studied through creep performance tests at 800 C/860C880 MPa, microstructure and
morphology observation, and dislocation configuration analyzation. It was found that, during the
creep process at the intermediate temperature, the α phase does not form a raft-like structure.
After a creep fracture, the distortion degree of the cubic α phase becomes greater when the
observation region is closer to the fracture. The alloy has a long creep life at 800 C, and the
dislocation slipping or climbing in the  matrix is the deformation mechanism at the early and middle
creep stages. At the later creep stage, the α phase is sheared by dislocations. Because of the low
stacking-fault energy of the alloy, the <110> superdislocation shearing into the α phase can
decompose on the {111} plane to form a (1/3) <112> partial dislocation and stacking-fault
configuration or cross-slip to the {100} plane to form the KearCWilsdorf (K-W) lock, which greatly
improves the creep resistance of the alloy. At the later creep stage, the primary/secondary slip
systems in the alloy are activated alternately, resulting in micro-cracks at the intersection of the
two slip systems. As the creep progresses, the initiated cracks spread and propagate in the  matrix
phase along a direction normal to the stress axis and connect with each other until creep fracture
occurs. This is the fracture mechanism of the alloy during creep at the medium temperature.

