Post-processing in additive manufacturing, particularly in laser powder bed fusion (LPBF), is
essential for optimizing the microstructure and achieving superior properties in nickel-based
superalloys. This study systematically investigates the effects of hot isostatic pressing (HIP)
temperature and subsequent heat treatment (HT) on the microstructure, recrystallization behavior,
and mechanical properties of IN718 superalloy produced via LPBF. The IN718 alloys underwent HIP at
three different temperatures (1120 °C, 1150 °C, and 1180 °C), followed by optimized HT. The findings
indicate that HIP effectively eliminates the Laves phase in the as-built microstructure. However,
variations in HIP temperature significantly influence recrystallization and microstructure.
Specifically, HIP at 1120 °C resulted in minimal recrystallization, thus retaining the cellular and
columnar structures of the as-built microstructure. At this temperature, γ″ precipitates with
various orientations and larger sizes formed at the grain boundaries, while smaller, unevenly
distributed γ″ precipitates were observed within the grains. As the HIP temperature increased to
1150 °C and 1180 °C, complete recrystallization occurred, transforming the columnar and cellular
structures into equiaxed grains. The γ″ precipitates became uniformly distributed within the grains
but were absent at the grain boundaries. Post-HIP followed by HT, the HIP1120 +HT alloy maintained
its columnar and cellular structures. It exhibited high-density spherical γ″ precipitates along
these substructure boundaries and more uniformly distributed fine γ″ precipitates within the grains.
The HIP1150 +HT and HIP1180 +HT alloys displayed a significant number of finer γ″ phases, uniformly
distributed within the grians; however, γ″ phases remained absent at the grain boundaries.
Additionally, the grain size of the HIP1180 +HT alloy notably increased. The HIP1120 +HT alloy
demonstrated the highest room-temperature and high-temperature tensile strength without sacrificing
ductility. This improvement is attributed to the cellular and columnar grains with finely
distributed high-density γ″ precipitates along the boundaries, which impede dislocation motion and
enhance strength, while preserving ductility by not completely hindering dislocation motion.

