Flow behavior and microstructures of superalloy 718 were investigated by hot compression tests
performed at temperatures ranging from 950 to 1100°C with strain rates of 10−3 to 1s−1. The
dependence of the peak stress on deformation temperature and strain rate can be expressed by a
hyperbolic-sine type equation. The activation energy for superalloy 718 is determined to be
443.2kJmol−1. A power exponent relationship between the peak strain and the Z parameter is obtained.
Microstructure analysis shows that the dynamically recrystallized grain size is inversely
proportional to the Z parameter. The nucleation mechanisms of DRX are closely related to the value
of Z parameter. Under low Z conditions, DRX nucleation and development are mainly assisted by the
formation of twins near the original grain boundaries.

