Considering the effects of the numbers and diameters of film cooling holes, the Low Cycle Fatigue
(LCF) performances of Ni-based single-crystal superalloy plate specimens were studied. The
investigation found that the low cycle fatigue lifetimes of the single hole specimens were
significantly longer than those of multi-holes specimens because of the multi-holes interference
effect. Combined with the rate dependent Crystal Plasticity (CP) theory and the Theory of Critical
Distances (TCD), the LCF lifetime prediction models of specimens with close packed film cooling
