Superalloys have been widely used to manufacture high-performance components of advanced aero-engine
and other high-tech products. To fabricate superalloy parts and structures, hot forming is an
efficient and prominent method widely employed to process this difficult-to-manufacture material. In
this article, some unique processing phenomena such as plastic flow characteristics and dynamic
recrystallization during hot forming of superalloys are discussed and presented. The main
manufacturing processes for making superalloy components are systematically introduced and
detailedly delineated, which include cogging, forging, ring rolling and powder metallurgy are
presented. The process fundamental and characteristics are also summarized to provide a basis for
advanced manufacturing of complex superalloy parts and structures. The detailed applications of
superalloy parts and structures are also exemplified. All of these aim at providing a panorama of
the hot forming of superalloys for making high-tech parts and structures for different industrial
clusters.

