In the current work, a type of Co-Al-W-based superalloy was fabricated via selective laser melting.
The samples were processed using two laser beam systems with different groups of processing
parameters. The first group produced a polycrystalline microstructure, while a macro-scale single
crystal (a cylinder of 2 mm diameter and 5 mm height) was first achieved by the second group. The
high stability of the melt pool and the lower flow rate of molten material under the second group of
processing parameters were the key factors for the formation of a single crystal, which was revealed
by the numerical analysis. Compared with the polycrystalline microstructure, the sample with single-
crystal microstructure had a higher hardness and compression yield strength in the room-temperature
nanoindentation tests.

