The purity of the Ni-based superalloy is profoundly influenced by the alloy-crucible interaction
during induction melting. Unraveling the interaction mechanism is pivotal for the fabrication of
high-purity Ni-based superalloys. In this study, the alloys were melted using the MgO crucibles
without and with the Ca addition, respectively. The results demonstrated that upon melting in the
Ca-free MgO crucible considerable dissolution and decomposition of MgO occurred. The oxygen released
from the decomposed MgO reacted with aluminum in the alloy to form Al2O3 product, which could
further react with the MgO matrix to generate the MgAl2O4 product. These products then floated to
the surface of the alloy, eventually forming a slag layer with a thickness of 80 μm. Upon the
addition of metallic Ca to the alloy melt, no slag layer was detected on the surface of the alloy.
This can be attributed to the fact that Ca could preferentially react with oxygen to form CaO,
leading to a decrease in the amount of Al2O3 products, and particularly, the generated CaO could
react with Al2O3 to form CaO-Al2O3 products. This reaction sequence completely inhibited the
reaction between Al2O3 and the MgO matrix, thereby preventing the formation of MgAl2O4 products. The
net effect is an enhancement in the purity of the alloys, which is of crucial importance for
augmenting its performance and quality.

