Oxygen-induced dynamic embrittlement of nickel (Ni)-base superalloys at 650°C has been studied by
static loading of notched bars in pure bending at fixed displacement on IN718 in the form of
conventionally heat-treated polycrystals, polycrystals specially processed to increase the fraction
of Σ3 boundaries, and bicrystals with a random high-angle boundary and a Σ5 tilt boundaries. The
susceptibility to cracking is shown to depend strongly on grain-boundary character.

