Lattice strain evolution is measured by neutron diffractrometry during tensile testing of a high
volume fraction polycrystal nickel superalloy. The intergranular strains reveal anisotropic
deformation, with the {200} plane being least stiff and the {111} being the stiffest. Initial
plasticity is due to yielding of the {220}. Deconvolution of the {200} and {220} doublets reveals
co-deformation of γ and γ′ phases during plasticity. Comparisons are drawn with low and medium
volume fraction superalloys.

