2026-01-26 18:30:48,283 [INFO] root - [50] 来源: Study on the inhibiting effect of pre-recovery heat treatment process control on recrystallization in Ni-based single crystal superalloy (块: None)
Ni-based single crystal superalloys have emerged as pivotal materials for turbine blade
manufacturing in aero engines, owing to their exceptional high-temperature mechanical properties.
However, the intricate preparation process often introduces residual stress, leading to
recrystallization defects during subsequent heat treatment and operational use. To mitigate this
issue, pre-recovery heat treatment, recognized as an effective method for alleviating internal
residual stress and suppressing recrystallization in the alloy, has been employed. In this study,
three distinct PHT schemes were designed, and the nanoindentation technique was employed to
quantitatively assess the residual stress in samples prior to standard solution treatment. The
results revealed that the critical residual stress range for recrystallization in DD6 single crystal
superalloy lies between 500 MPa and 650 MPa. The designed schemes (isothermal pre-recovery heat
treatment, cyclic pre-recovery heat treatment, and heating pre-recovery heat treatment) were found
to effectively inhibit recrystallization in the single crystal superalloy subjected to 3 % room-
temperature uniaxial compressive plastic strain. Notably, the cyclic pre-recovery heat treatment
scheme, characterized by temperature fluctuations during the thermal cycling process, promotes the
annihilation of a larger number of dislocations during their movement. Consequently, the most
effective recrystallization-inhibiting scheme was identified as the cyclic pre-recovery heat
treatment, successfully preventing recrystallization in superalloys subjected to 5 % plastic strain.
This research provides a more practical method for reducing the manufacturing cost of single crystal
superalloy turbine blades.

