The integrity of turbine blades is at the heart of every operating aerospace engine or land-based
gas turbine. In the most demanding environments, they are cast as single crystals. Whereas
traditional neutron imaging is an excellent tool for the non-destructive testing in search of cracks
or residual core material, it is insensitive to variations in crystallographic properties. In this
work we show that by simultaneous imaging of the diffracted neutrons, one can also probe variations
in crystal orientation. In a matter of seconds, different dendritic growth in two industrial turbine
blades could be ascertained.

