2026-01-26 18:42:04,379 [INFO] root - [17] 来源: Development of heat-treatment process for a P/M superalloy for turbine blades (块: None)
Powder metallurgy (P/M) superalloy turbine blades are increasingly being used in jet-aircraft
engines. A notable advancement in the manufacture of P/M turbine blades is the development of a
powder forge IN-792 nickel-based superalloy: APK-6. Although fatigue strength of the P/M material is
212 times superior as compared to cast blades, its creep strength is poor. The improvement in creep
strength is ensured though microstructural control achieved by developing a suitable heat-treatment
process.Heat-treatment experiments in the temperature range of 117–1270°C for duration of 20min–22h
are conducted. The microstructural characterization involved use of both optical as well as electron
microscopes (SEM). Appropriate microstructural features (grain size and γ′ particles size) in the
P/M material were achieved through the grain-growth heat-treatment experiments so as to render the
material suitable for application in turbine blades of aircraft engines.

2026-01-26 18:42:04,379 [INFO] root - [18] 来源: Microstructural Analysis of DV – 2 Ni – Base Superalloy Turbine Blade after High Temperature Damage (块: None)
High pressure turbine (HPT) blades of DV – 2 jet engines are made from Ni – based superalloy. This
alloy was originally manufactured in the Soviet Union and referred as ŽS6K. For improving alloy's
high temperature resistance are blades coated with Al – Si protective layer. A regular operation
temperature of HPT blades vary from 705°C to 750°C. An overcrossing working temperature range causes
degradation of the protective coating as well as base material. Article deals with such case, when
working temperature of HPT blades were overcrossed and provide microstructural analysis of Al – Si
coating and base material as well.

2026-01-26 18:42:04,379 [INFO] root - [19] 来源: Microstructural Investigation of Stress-Induced Degradation of Gamma and Gamma Prime Phases on the Surface of the Aerofoil of Nickel-Based Single Crystal Superalloy Turbine Blades (块: None)
Nickel-based single-crystal superalloy turbine blades are typically manufactured via investment
casting followed by a well-established heat treatment process, resulting in a uniform microstructure
composed of thin  channels and cubic-shaped α. However, the region near the corner of the
aerofoil/platform of the blade exhibits a distinct contrast compared to the surrounding area. High-
resolution scanning electron microscopy (SEM) reveals significant degradation of the  and α phases
in the dark contrast region. In this area, the α phase no longer maintains its characteristic cubic
morphology and appears partially dissolved or even melted. Although the regularity of the /α
microstructure is disrupted, the region is still composed of irregular-shaped  and α phases. Based
on these microstructural observations, a possible formation mechanism of the abnormal microstructure
is discussed. Although the blades are not exposed to conventional creep conditions during casting
and heat treatment, residual stress accumulated during casting may be relieved at elevated
temperatures during the heat treatment process. The synergistic effect of stress, temperature, and
time may contribute to the formation of the observed abnormal microstructure.

