The dendritic Ni3Al based single crystal superalloy of [001] crystal orientation was prepared at
constant (50, 100 and 200μm/s) and changing withdrawal rates (from 100μm/s to 50μm/s, and from
200μm/s to 50μm/s). It was found that the growth history plays significant role in the primary
dendrite arm spacing in the ‘as cast’ state, while its influence on the dendrite orientation was
little. It is stated that the spatial orientation was the basic factor of intermediate temperature
stress rupture properties of complete solution treated alloys, i.e. the samples with orientation
