The effects of Al and Ti on the thermal expansion coefficient (TEC) of a NiCFeCCr-based superalloy,
for the application of advanced ultra-supercritical (A-USC) systems, were investigated by combining
the experiments and first-principles calculations. The results imply that MC/M23C6 carbides and
grain boundary seem to play a minor role in the TEC of alloys due to their relatively low
proportions. Al and Ti elements increase the TEC of alloys when present in  matrix, but they
decrease the TEC value by taking part in the formation of α phase. When the amount of α phase is
low, the TEC of alloys at 600C700°C is dominated by  matrix, and consequently shows an increasing or
constant trend with the increase of Al?+?Ti content. Replacing Al element with Ti element decreases
the TEC of alloys in two ways: directly by reducing the TECs of  matrix and α phase, and indirectly
by increasing the amount of α phase. These results can provide the theoretical basis and beneficial
guidelines for the composition design and optimization of low-expansion superalloys.

