A Gaussian process regression model for estimating the creep rupture stress of single crystal
nickel-based superalloys is constructed. It is built and validated on data disclosed in patents as
well as scientific and technical reports. This model is coupled with computational thermodynamics
for the prediction of microstructural features, and a model for the estimation of density. Using
this combination, the key characteristics of a large number of potential alloys are systematically
computed as a function of their composition. Materials specifications targeted towards applications
as turbine blades, which none of the alloys from the creep database follow, narrow down the search
from 300,000 000 to 180,000 alloys. A large number of these candidates are predicted as featuring
greater specific properties than existing single crystal nickel-based superalloys. The selection
criteria used to isolate alloys for experimental validation are discussed, and lead to several
alloys displaying good agreement between their predicted microstructure and estimated properties.

2026-01-26 19:30:13,941 [INFO] root - [49] 来源: Improved oxidation resistance and excellent strength of nickel-based superalloy at 1100℃ by determining critical Cr-Al value (块: None)
The oxidation resistance and high temperature strength of superalloys cannot coexist when they are
used above 1000℃. The lowest critical value of Cr-Al for the formation of continuous Al2O3 layer to
improve the oxidation resistance of superalloy at 1100℃ was determined on the basis of ensuring the
excellent high temperature strength on Ni-18Co-18 W matrix. The alloy exhibits improved oxidation
resistance at an optimal value of 18Cr3.5Al. In addition, the experiment confirmed that the strength
improvement can be achieved by reducing Cr and increasing the solid solubility of W. The tensile
strength of these alloys is similar to that of Haynes 230 and Haynes 188. However, the rupture life
of 18Cr3.5Al alloy at 1000℃/40 MPa reaches 115 h, better than Haynes 230 and Haynes 188. The results
provide a definite reference value of Cr-Al for improving the properties of alloys.

