The microstructure of a nickel-based single crystal superalloy has been investigated after creep
deformation at 1033 K under stress up to 850 MPa. High density of stacking faults are formed in γ′
precipitates during the creep deformation. At 700 MPa and 850 MPa, bi-directional stacking faults
are observed. These stacking faults are induced by the movement of a/3<112> partial dislocations.
Two formation mechanisms of a/3<112> partial dislocations, reaction and dissociation mechanisms, are
simultaneously observed in this alloy after deformation. The proportion of a/3<112> dislocations
formed by the dissociation mechanism increases as the applied stress increases. The primary creep
strain of this alloy is 2.45% at 850 MPa, which may be attributed to the combination of these two
mechanisms.

