The stress rupture mechanism of turbine blades under high-temperature sequential loading (including
high → low (H → L) and low → high (L → H)) is crucial for aeroengine safety. This study
experimentally investigates the notch strengthening effect of single-crystal nickel-based superalloy
DD6 under continuous and sequential creep loading at 980 °C. Deformation and fracture were revealed
using Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) and Electron Backscatter Diffraction (EBSD) on interrupted
and failed specimens. Compared to smooth specimens, V-type notched specimens exhibited a significant
increase in stress rupture life, up to 12 times longer, demonstrating a notable notch strengthening
effect. The triaxial stress state induced by the notch geometry limits local deformation, suppresses
crack propagation, and prolongs material durability. The rafting type of γ′ phase at the notch root
depends on the direction of maximum principal stress. As stress concentration increases, γ matrix
channels within slip bands become narrower. Narrower γ-phase channels increase resistance to
dislocation motion and suppress slip band activity while reducing deformation rate to enhance the
notch strengthening effect. Compared to continuous loading, sequential loading of notched specimens
results in a significant sequential effect. Under H → L loading, voids concentrate internally,
whereas L → H loading accelerates damage accumulation through internal and surface defect
interactions. H → L loading mitigates damage through stress relaxation and triaxial constraints,
exhibiting a strengthening effect. Conversely, L → H loading accelerates void coalescence and crack
instability, leading to a weakening effect.

