Creep/fatigue accelerated failures of K403 superalloy turbine blades are inspected, and the damage
behaviors are revealed and failure modes are determined in laboratory. It is concluded that: (1)
significant discrepancies of fractographies and cross-sectional microstructures are interpreted by
qualitative and quantitative investigations; (2) the observed morphologies are mainly attributed to
the dendrite separation and γ' phase rafting behaviors, together with the development of ‘void
migration mechanism’, i.e., grain interior → grain boundary → sub grain boundary; (3) the failure of
turbine blade changes from the mixed to the intergranular mode, which is essentially controlled by
the contribution proportion of creep damage and fatigue damage.

