Turbine blades are mainly subjected to thermal stress and gradient centrifugal stress in operational
conditions. The effects of centrifugal stress on the microstructural degradation of single crystal
superalloys under a multiaxial stress state remain unclear. Here, the NiAlReRu component was exposed
to high-speed rotation at 20,000?rpm and a high temperature of 1000°C for 100?h. The analysis
indicates that the centrifugal stress causes the distortion of dendrite stems and the aggregation of
micropores. The centrifugal stress promotes the formation of interfacial dislocation networks,
accompanied by more severe rafting of α phases. These findings provide a comprehensive experimental
understanding of the microstructural evolution under simulated turbine blade working conditions,
which provides a reference for the subsequent tests under near-service conditions.

