New generation internally cooled turbine blades for large land-based gas turbines for electrical
power supply have cooling systems which are as complicated as those used in the small turbine blades
of gas turbine aircraft engines. This leads to a very complex temperature and hence stress
distribution in the component. A simplified model blade with three cooling channels has been
developed in order to study the cyclic thermal mechanical behaviour. A specially designed high
frequency induction coil with a maximum electrical power input 60 kW has been developed for the
heating system. The difficulties are discussed of obtaining temperature distributions in model
blades which are as close as possible to those encountered in real components. The temperature of
the model blade was measured by 12 thermocouples and the temperature gradients calculated by
interpolation between the thermocouples and by estimation the heat transfer across the wall
thickness. For different thermal cycles, the temperature distributions estimated by FE calculations
are discussed. In addition the model blade was subjected to a constant load to simulate centrifugal
forces. The strain development could only measured with extensometer fixed at the trailing edge of
the blade within the heated zone of the model blade. The stress and strain behaviour was evaluated
using the ABAQUS-FE program for elastic and inelastical analysis.

