US Bureau of Mines researchers have developed a process to recover high-purity cobalt and nickel
from superalloy scrap (SAS). The process utilizes novel, Bureau-developed double-membrane
electrolytic cells to electrorefine SAS into high-purity cobalt cathodes and high-purity nickel
cathodes. The cells anodically dissolve carburized SAS producing a chloride anolyte. This anolyte is
treated by a series of hydrometallurgical provedures which yield chromium chemicals and purified
cobalt chloride s and nickel chloride solutions. The purified solutions are utilized as catholytes
