superalloy’s machining layer can be modified into a porous layer with low strength and hardness,
making the machining easier. The modified layer thickness, as a critical factor in determining
surface modification’s effectiveness, should be accurately predicted. However, relevant research has
not been studied. In this paper, a modified layer thickness prediction model was established for the
first time through the combination of theoretical analysis and experimental study. Three key
electrochemical parameters, including potential, electrolyte concentration, and polarization time,
were considered in the model. The predicted value of modified layer thickness is in good agreement
with the experimental value, verifying the feasibility of the model. The model reveals that the
potential, electrolyte concentration, and polarization time are positively correlated with the
modified layer thickness. The importance of these three electrochemical parameters on modified layer
thickness is ordered as: potential > polarization time > electrolyte concentration. This study
provides a basis for improving the predictability of γ-electrochemical selective dissolution surface
modification technique.

