Microalloying is a cost-effective strategy to develop high performance nickel-based superalloys.
Here, the mechanisms of rare earth Y in IN713C on purification, mechanical property improvement, and
microstructure modification were systematically investigated. Via melt purification of Y, the O, N,
and S elements are reduced to 5?ppm in the IN713C alloy. By adding Y to 74?ppm, the yield strength,
tensile strength, and elongation of the IN713C alloy at 650°C are increased by 12.2%, 14.5%, and
66%, respectively. Microstructure analysis indicated that the strength of the alloy is improved by
refining the size and increasing the volume fraction of the α phase. The plasticity of the alloy is
improved by modifying the morphology and distribution of MC carbides. The modification of carbide
morphology can be attributed to the enrichment of Y at the surface of carbides and the change of
segregation of Nb, which is the main carbide-formation element.

