2026-01-26 21:25:24,758 [INFO] root - Reduce阶段：筛选出 4 个高质量关键点，内容如下：[{'answer': 'The columnar-to-equiaxed transition (CET) is expected to depend on growth velocity and temperature gradient as follows: (i) Higher solidification velocities tend to promote equiaxed grain formation by increasing undercooling and reducing the stability of columnar dendrites; (ii) Lower thermal gradients similarly favor equiaxed nucleation by decreasing the directional solidification driving force, allowing stray grains—potentially nucleated on particles like TiN—to develop. CFD simulations confirm that both solidification velocity and thermal gradient are key inputs that align with the CET curve, indicating their critical role in determining microstructure evolution [cite: chunk-6532d87c8e60a5c4bab54616c8dfaa78-e-14, chunk-6532d87c8e60a5c4bab54616c8dfaa78-e-19, chunk-6532d87c8e60a5c4bab54616c8dfaa78-e-18, chunk-6532d87c8e60a5c4bab54616c8dfaa78-e-20, chunk-6532d87c8e60a5c4bab54616c8dfaa78-r-14, chunk-6532d87c8e60a5c4bab54616c8dfaa78-r-15, chunk-6532d87c8e60a5c4bab54616c8dfaa78-r-16, chunk-6532d87c8e60a5c4bab54616c8dfaa78-r-17].', 'score': 9}, {'answer': "CFD-calculated thermal gradients and solidification velocities are directly linked to the columnar-to-equiaxed transition (CET) curve through 'AGREES_WITH' relationships, demonstrating that these parameters govern the conditions under which equiaxed grains—potentially nucleated on TiN particles—can form ahead of the solid/liquid interface [cite: chunk-6532d87c8e60a5c4bab54616c8dfaa78-e-19, chunk-6532d87c8e60a5c4bab54616c8dfaa78-e-18, chunk-6532d87c8e60a5c4bab54616c8dfaa78-e-20, chunk-6532d87c8e60a5c4bab54616c8dfaa78-r-16, chunk-6532d87c8e60a5c4bab54616c8dfaa78-r-17].", 'score': 8}, {'answer': 'The integration of CFD outputs with the minimum undercooling criterion suggests that nucleation of equiaxed grains (e.g., on TiN particles) is thermodynamically controlled; both high growth velocity and low temperature gradient enhance undercooling, thereby promoting CET—a mechanism supported by the multi-physics modeling framework described [cite: chunk-6532d87c8e60a5c4bab54616c8dfaa78-e-14, chunk-6532d87c8e60a5c4bab54616c8dfaa78-e-15, chunk-6532d87c8e60a5c4bab54616c8dfaa78-r-11].', 'score': 8}, {'answer': 'The columnar-to-equiaxed transition (CET) is expected to be strongly influenced by both growth velocity and temperature gradient. A lower temperature gradient reduces the directional solidification driving force, promoting equiaxed grain formation by enabling constitutional supercooling ahead of the interface. Higher growth velocities can suppress CET by limiting solute diffusion and reducing the time for nucleation events; however, if TiN particles act as potent nucleants ahead of the interface—as suggested in the query—their presence may offset this suppression, especially at moderate velocities where undercooling is sufficient for heterogeneous nucleation. Thus, CET is favored at low temperature gradients and intermediate growth velocities that balance undercooling and nucleation kinetics [cite: chunk-e5bb332f13c6ee8ccbd4a405a9eb9b87-e-6, chunk-e5bb332f13c6ee8ccbd4a405a9eb9b87-e-7, chunk-e5bb332f13c6ee8ccbd4a405a9eb9b87-r-5].', 'score': 8}]
