A new evaluation method for mixed-grain structures caused by incomplete recrystallization during the
ring-rolling of superalloys has been established. This method can accurately predict the
microstructure uniformity of ring forgings, demonstrating good agreement with experimental results.
By applying the established method, the influence of initial grain size on the microstructure
uniformity of ring forgings was systematically analyzed. The results revealed that the initial grain
size condition can be assessed more rigorously, leading to significantly improved microstructure
uniformity in the final forging. Additionally, this evaluation method is not only applicable to the
analysis of initial grain size but can also be extended to investigate the effects of other process
parameters. Furthermore, it holds potential for broader applications in other hot deformation
processes, such as cogging or die forging.

