2026-01-26 22:31:02,030 [INFO] root - Reduce阶段：筛选出 4 个高质量关键点，内容如下：[{'answer': 'The probability of successfully detecting a defect during ultrasonic non-destructive testing depends strongly on grain size because uniform and fine grain structures reduce acoustic scattering, leading to clearer signals and improved defect detectability. Powder metallurgy (P/M) billets have a significant advantage because they exhibit uniform grain size and reduced segregation—microstructural traits directly produced by the Powder Metallurgy process—which enhance inspectability compared to conventional cast-and-wrought products [cite: chunk-100afba087a43fc5308b3284aa10cb35-e-4, chunk-100afba087a43fc5308b3284aa10cb35-e-6, chunk-100afba087a43fc5308b3284aa10cb35-r-2, chunk-100afba087a43fc5308b3284aa10cb35-e-8, chunk-100afba087a43fc5308b3284aa10cb35-e-7, chunk-100afba087a43fc5308b3284aa10cb35-r-3, chunk-100afba087a43fc5308b3284aa10cb35-r-4, chunk-0365022a18454a90e4598122a8f449ca-e-1, chunk-0365022a18454a90e4598122a8f449ca-r-1, chunk-0365022a18454a90e4598122a8f449ca-r-2]', 'score': 9}, {'answer': 'Grain size strongly influences ultrasonic non-destructive testing because smaller or more uniform grain structures reduce acoustic scattering, improving defect detectability. Powder-processed billets typically exhibit finer and more homogeneous grain sizes compared to conventionally forged billets, which often have coarse, irregular grains that scatter ultrasonic waves and mask defects. This microstructural advantage enhances signal clarity in powder billets during inspection. [cite: chunk-c170c36341964554c175d2ca279d86c6-e-10, chunk-3a81ceaec05233057c8b81a8b4a57416-e-9, chunk-3a81ceaec05233057c8b81a8b4a57416-r-6]', 'score': 9}, {'answer': 'Ultrasonic non-destructive testing (NDT) effectiveness is highly sensitive to grain size because larger grains scatter ultrasonic waves, reducing signal clarity and defect detectability. Powder billets, such as those made from Rene 104 powder superalloy, typically exhibit fine and uniform ASTM grain sizes, which minimize ultrasonic scattering and enhance defect detection reliability. This fine grain structure is a key reason powder metallurgy billets offer a significant advantage over conventionally forged billets with coarser or more variable grain structures in NDT inspections. [cite: chunk-c59c4008e266198b10130c9d3679b582-e-1, chunk-c59c4008e266198b10130c9d3679b582-e-2, chunk-c59c4008e266198b10130c9d3679b582-r-1, chunk-c59c4008e266198b10130c9d3679b582-e-4, chunk-c59c4008e266198b10130c9d3679b582-r-3, chunk-27e744dfd57dfdad9eeacf1afe66f2e9-e-1, chunk-27e744dfd57dfdad9eeacf1afe66f2e9-r-1]', 'score': 9}, {'answer': 'Fine grain size, such as ASTM 9, generally improves mechanical properties like strength and toughness by impeding dislocation motion and crack propagation. In ultrasonic non-destructive testing (NDT), smaller grain sizes reduce acoustic scattering, which enhances the signal-to-noise ratio and increases the probability of detecting actual defects. Coarse-grained materials scatter ultrasonic waves more significantly, masking defect signals. Powder-processed billets typically exhibit much finer and more uniform grain structures compared to conventionally forged superalloy billets, which often have coarse, heterogeneous grains. This fine microstructure in powder billets thus provides a significant advantage for reliable ultrasonic inspection. [cite: E1]', 'score': 8}]
