cladding. The characteristics of the thermal barrier coating were evaluated based on results from
optical and electron microscopy images, X-ray diffraction, and microhardness tests. Optical and
electron microscopy images showed that the ceramic layer consists of a cellular structure in the
outer layer and a planar structure at the interface with the bond coat. Moreover, the
microstructural morphology at the interface between the bond coat and the top layer displayed
cellular structures and epitaxial growth, which result from the laser remelting and resolidification
of the bond coat during the deposition of the top coat. Map and line EDS results at the interface
between the bond coat and the top layer indicate that the Al and Mo elements diffused into the YSZ-
deposited layer, while Zr, Y, and O diffused into the NiCoCrAlY-deposited layer. YSZ coatings
demonstrated significantly higher microhardness compared to NiCoCrAlY and substrate due to the ZrO2
phase. NiCoCrAlY coatings showed increased hardness at interfaces due to the diffusion of elements
such as Mo and Zr, which improves overall performance and durability in gas turbine applications.

