2026-01-26 23:00:06,176 [INFO] root - [49] 来源: Investigations on the Water-Jet Guided Laser drilling film cooling holes on the 8.5 mm-thick TBC superalloy (块: None)
With the escalating performance demands of aviation engines, a prevalent approach involves applying
ceramic thermal barrier coatings (TBCs) on the surfaces of nickel-based alloy blades and drilling
film cooling holes (FCHs) is undertaken to augment the high-temperature operational capacity. In
order to achieve high-quality and high-efficiency drilling of FCHs on large-thickness TBC nickel-
based alloy with small taper and limited defects, this paper utilizes the water-jet guided laser
(WJGL) technology that couples a high-energy nanosecond laser with a high-velocity waterjet.
Firstly, an energy coupling model between the TBC alloy and WJGL was constructed based on transient
heat conduction theory and deformation geometry methods, from which the cooling effect of water jet
is elucidated. The origins of shoulder-type defects arising during the machining process are
systematically analyzed, and the defects were mitigated through optimizing the concentric circular
scanning trajectory. Upon this foundation, orthogonal experiments were conducted to analyze the
influence of various processing parameters on the roundness and taper of the FCHs, and the optimal
parameter set for machining vertical FCHs was identified. Subsequent to experimentation aimed at the
creation of inclined FCHs, an in-depth analysis was undertaken to discern the factors contributing
to both acute-edge material fragmentation and sidewall damage. Through meticulous optimization of
the machining procedures, high-quality 45° inclined FCHs with a deep-diameter ratio of 20:1, minimal
taper, coating fragmentation and delamination, as well as heat-affected zones (HAZs) were
successfully fabricated on an 8.5 mm-thick TBC alloy.

2026-01-26 23:00:06,176 [INFO] root - [50] 来源: Effect of the anti-seepage masking layer on the microstructure evolution of Ni-based superalloy during co-deposition of the Al-Si coating (块: None)
This study optimizes turbine blade microstructures during production, specifically focusing on
surface treatments for blade airfoils and dovetails. Blade airfoils need to prepare the Al-Si
coating to improve oxidation resistance, while blade dovetails must prepare the anti-seepage masking
layer to hinder the deposition and diffusion of active atoms into the substrate. The effect of the
anti-seepage masking layer on the microstructure evolution of IN718 superalloys during co-deposition
of the Al-Si coating was systematically revealed. Results indicate that by adding Si elements to
modify the aluminide coating, a uniform and dense Al-Si coating was prepared on IN718 superalloys at
950 ℃ for blade airfoils, enhancing oxidation resistance. Meanwhile, the anti-seepage masking layer
with 42 µm could effectively prevent the deposition and diffusion of Al and Si atoms into the
substrate for blade dovetails. Defects such as interface layers, micro-voids, and cracks between the
masking layer and substrate facilitate the removal of the anti-seepage masking layer from the
substrate after heat treatment. On the contrary, the thicker anti-seepage masking layer will induce
the upward diffusion of Cr and Mo atoms and form a stronger metallurgical bonding with the
substrate, which is difficult to remove and will harm the properties of the substrate.

