Metallic coatings are widely employed to improve the oxidation resistance of superalloys. However,
the interdiffusion between the metallic coatings and the superalloys leads to microstructural
degradation in both. Some of the underlying degradation mechanisms are still elusive, e.g., the α
(Ni3Al) phase depletion in superalloys, where a large amount of α precipitates are dissolved in the
matrix even though the incoming Al from coatings indeed increases the Al content. Here, we
investigated the interdiffusion behavior between the Amdry365 coating and the IN792 superalloy at
1100°C, using multiple microscopic techniques and thermodynamics calculations. Our results showed an
excellent agreement between experiments and thermodynamics simulations, indicating the dominant role
of Al on the initial diffusion-induced phase transitions. We proposed the Al-Cr interference effect
to account for the pile-up behavior of Cr and the reduced Al content near the coating/superalloy
interface. The local phase equilibrium calculations revealed that the α depletion in the superalloy
is primarily attributed to the loss of α-forming elements, such as Ta and Ti. Our findings opened up
an avenue for studies on the superalloy/coating interdiffusion, contributing to reducing this
damaging impact.

