Al, Al-Si and Cr-Al-Si coatings are prepared onto IC20 alloy by powder pack cementation to improve
the oxidation resistance of the alloy. The isothermal oxidation behaviors of the coatings and the
uncoated IC20 alloy at 1,100 °C are comparatively investigated. For the coatings, less weight gains
are obtained compared to the uncoated alloy after 100 h thermal exposure. The Al-Si coating exhibits
the best oxidation resistance, while the addition of Cr accelerates the scale spallation. Phase
transformation from β-NiAl to γ and γ′ occurs in the coating after oxidation for extended periods.
The oxidation protection and degradation mechanisms for these coatings are discussed.

