Oxidation behavior and its effect on tensile properties of a powder metallurgy EP962NP superalloy
have been investigated. Results show that the oxidation resistance of studied alloy has been
improved by hot isostatic pressing as compared to the as-cast one. Oxidation process of the alloy
powder metallurgy EP962NP is limited by diffusion of Cr3+ due to oxidation activation energy of
244.1 kJ·mol−1. In addition, the main reason for cracking and spalling during steady-state oxidation
can be inferred as the rapid increase in growth stress caused by spinel. Interestingly, the tensile
samples oxidized at 900 °C exhibit improved plasticity due to the refinement of γ′ caused by octet
splitting.

