NiCoCrAlY coating that has been oxidized at high temperature is successfully removed by
electrochemical stripping from a nickel-based superalloy, which is followed by recoating a fresh
one. Then oxidation behavior of the recoated NiCoCrAlY coating is investigated in comparison with
the ordinary one. Results indicate that the recoated coating exhibits a lower oxidation rate with a
rate constant of 3.1×10−14  g2 cm-4s-1 at 1000 °C. However, its oxide scale is more likely to spall
off than that of the ordinary one in cyclic oxidation. Elements interdiffusion between the NiCoCrAlY
coatings and the alloy substrate leads to the different oxidation behavior.

